{"meta":{"course":"inst-design","chapter":"02","section":"04","chapter_title":"ID Process Models","title":"Module 4: Beyond ADDIE — Comparing ID Process Models","description":"Compare Dick and Carey, the Kemp model, Smith and Ragan, SAM, and Agile/Lean ID, then learn when to choose each.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"inst-design-02-04-lo-01","statement":"Distinguish at least four ID process models by their distinguishing emphasis"},{"id":"inst-design-02-04-lo-02","statement":"Match a project's risk, uncertainty, and audience to an appropriate model choice"},{"id":"inst-design-02-04-lo-03","statement":"Explain why mature designers combine an alignment model with a delivery model"}],"concept_ids":["id_models","dick_and_carey_model","kemp_model","smith_and_ragan_model","successive_approximation_model","agile_id"],"prereq_section_ids":["inst-design/02/03"],"practice_ids":["id_models_dnc_strength","id_models_kemp_pattern","id_models_sam_fit","id_models_combine"],"estimated_time_minutes":12,"order":4,"lo_practice_map":{"inst-design-02-04-lo-01":["id_models_dnc_strength"],"inst-design-02-04-lo-02":["id_models_kemp_pattern","id_models_sam_fit"],"inst-design-02-04-lo-03":["id_models_combine"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Beyond ADDIE: Comparing ID Process Models\n\n<figure-block id=\"section-anchor-inst-design-02-04\" caption=\"Learning map for Module 4: Beyond ADDIE — Comparing ID Process Models: principle, evidence, and transfer.\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/section-anchors/inst-design/02-04.svg\" alt=\"Learning map for Module 4: Beyond ADDIE — Comparing ID Process Models, connecting id models, dick and carey model, kemp model to evidence and transfer.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n\n\n> **\"A model is a story you tell yourself about how the work will go. The danger is forgetting it is a story.\"**\n\n---\n\n## Opening: One Process Did Not Fit\n\nA learning-and-development lead at a hospital network was asked to build two things in the same quarter. The first was a 6-month certification course for nurses on a new infusion-pump protocol, where a single wrong instruction could harm a patient and where state auditors would later inspect the assessment. The second was a 20-minute refresher on a new scheduling app, where the requirements were vague, the deadline was three weeks out, and nobody could say in advance what \"good\" looked like.\n\nThe lead's instinct was to run both projects through the same trusted process, ADDIE, executed front to back. The certification course came out strong. The scheduling refresher came out late, over-documented, and still wrong, because the team spent two of their three weeks writing an analysis report for a problem that only became clear once a rough prototype was in front of real users.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** The failure was not ADDIE. It was treating one process model as the answer to every kind of project. Different projects carry different risks (accuracy, speed, ambiguity, feasibility), and the right process model is the one that attacks the dominant risk first.\n\nThis module compares the major instructional design process models and gives you a defensible way to choose among them.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Distinguish** at least four ID process models by their defining emphasis and underlying assumptions.\n2. **Match** a project's risk, uncertainty, and audience to an appropriate model choice.\n3. **Apply** a model-selection frame to a concrete, multi-constraint project.\n4. **Explain** why mature designers run an alignment model and a delivery model at the same time.\n\n---\n\n## Why a Single Model Is Not Enough\n\nWhen most people first meet instructional design, they meet ADDIE: Analyze, Design, Develop, Implement, Evaluate. Five clean phases, often drawn as a linear arrow. ADDIE is a useful shared vocabulary, but treating it as the only ID model produces brittle designers. Real projects rarely march left to right through the five letters. They iterate, skip back, and run phases in parallel.\n\nInstructional design models are best understood as **lenses**, not as recipes. Each one foregrounds a different concern: systematic alignment, learner-centered design, rapid iteration, classroom feasibility, or organizational performance. Picking the right model, or knowing when to mix several, is part of professional judgment.\n\nA helpful way to organize the field is along two axes. The first axis is **how much the model front-loads specification** (heavy analysis and documentation up front versus learning by building). The second axis is **how the model treats change** (change as a defect to be controlled versus change as the expected mode of progress). Where a model sits on those two axes tells you the kind of project it serves well.\n\n| Axis | Front-loaded / Control change | Build-to-learn / Embrace change |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Models** | Dick & Carey, Smith & Ragan | SAM, Agile/Lean |\n| **Strength** | Traceability, defensibility, alignment | Speed, responsiveness, learner signal |\n| **Risk it attacks** | Wrong objectives/assessment slipping through | Building the wrong thing in detail |\n| **Fails when** | Requirements are still unknown | Accuracy must be guaranteed pre-release |\n\nKemp sits in the middle: structured like the alignment models, but explicitly non-linear like the iterative ones.\n\n---\n\n## Dick, Carey, and Carey: The Systems View\n\nThe **Dick, Carey, and Carey model** (often shortened to \"Dick and Carey\") is the most explicitly systems-oriented model in mainstream ID. Its signature move is to treat instruction as a tightly coupled system in which a change to one component requires recalculation of the others.\n\nThe model has nine interacting components:\n\n1. Identify instructional goals\n2. Conduct instructional analysis (including subordinate skills)\n3. Analyze learners and contexts\n4. Write performance objectives\n5. Develop assessment instruments\n6. Develop instructional strategy\n7. Develop and select materials\n8. Design and conduct formative evaluation\n9. Revise instruction (then conduct summative evaluation)\n\nThe strong contribution of Dick and Carey is **alignment discipline**. Objectives, assessment, and strategy are designed in lockstep, and any drift between them is treated as a defect. Teams that struggle with assessment-objective drift, mismatched item types, or vague performance criteria benefit most from this model. The deliberate ordering, objectives (step 4) before assessment (step 5) before strategy (step 6), is what forces backward design: you cannot write a test before you know the objective, and you cannot pick a teaching strategy before you know what the test will demand.\n\nIts weakness is also its strength: the model can feel heavy for short-cycle work. A 30-minute microlearning module rarely needs a full subordinate-skills hierarchy, and forcing one wastes the budget on documentation nobody will read.\n\n---\n\n## The Kemp Model: Continuous and Non-Linear\n\nThe **Kemp model** (also associated with Morrison, Ross, Kalman, and Kemp) reorganizes ID work into nine elements arranged in an oval rather than a sequence. The elements include instructional problems, learner characteristics, task analysis, instructional objectives, content sequencing, instructional strategies, message design, instructional delivery, and evaluation instruments. Surrounding the oval are continuous activities such as planning, project management, support services, and formative evaluation.\n\nThe Kemp model captures something honest about real ID work:\n\n- You can start at any element where you have new information.\n- You revisit earlier elements whenever a downstream decision exposes a gap.\n- Project management, budgeting, and stakeholder communication run continuously, not as a separate phase.\n\nKemp is especially well suited for **classroom and instructional designers in K-12 and higher education**, where the work is rarely linear and where the designer often also teaches the course. A teacher redesigning a unit might begin at \"content sequencing\" because the textbook changed, then loop back to \"instructional objectives\" when the new sequence reveals a missing prerequisite. Kemp legitimizes that loop instead of treating it as a process violation.\n\n---\n\n## Smith and Ragan: A Designer-Friendly Decomposition\n\nThe **Smith and Ragan model** is closer in spirit to ADDIE, but with a sharper focus on the cognitive psychology that justifies design decisions. Their three macro phases are Analysis, Strategy, and Evaluation, and within each they expose concrete decision points the designer must defend.\n\nFor example, under Strategy, Smith and Ragan distinguish between:\n\n- **Organizational strategy** (sequencing and structuring of content)\n- **Delivery strategy** (medium, mode, and grouping of learners)\n- **Management strategy** (pacing, learner control, and progress monitoring)\n\nThis separation matters because many novice designers conflate all three into one decision. They pick \"a video course\" and treat that single choice as if it answered sequencing, medium, and pacing at once. Smith and Ragan explicitly tell you to design the three separately and then verify their interaction, because a delivery choice (self-paced video) can quietly undermine an organizational choice (tightly scaffolded sequence) if pacing is left entirely to the learner.\n\nThe model is widely used in graduate ID programs because it scaffolds **why** decisions are made, not just what to do.\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"id-model-comparison\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## SAM: The Successive Approximation Model\n\nThe **Successive Approximation Model (SAM)**, developed by Michael Allen and colleagues, was created in direct response to perceived rigidity in ADDIE. SAM has three phases:\n\n1. **Preparation**: quick gathering of background, goals, and constraints\n2. **Iterative Design**: short cycles of prototyping, review, and revision\n3. **Iterative Development**: implementation cycles that continue to refine the working product\n\nThe cultural shift behind SAM is large. SAM expects that:\n\n- Stakeholders cannot fully specify what they want before they see something working.\n- Early prototypes will be wrong, and that is the point.\n- Decisions are made by testing artifacts, not by signing off on documents.\n\nSAM is the right choice when **requirements are unclear, time is short, and rework is cheaper than over-specification**. It is a poor choice when high-stakes accuracy must be guaranteed before any learner sees the material, for example regulated medical or aviation training where a wrong prototype released too early creates real risk. The cure for that limitation is to keep SAM's iteration but route prototypes through SME and compliance review before any learner exposure.\n\n---\n\n## Agile and Lean ID\n\nA growing body of practice borrows from software development. **Agile ID** treats the course as a set of small, deliverable learning chunks shipped in sprints. **Lean ID** treats every design decision as a hypothesis to be validated by learner data.\n\nAgile and Lean share several commitments:\n\n- **Working learning experiences over comprehensive design documents.**\n- **Learner feedback loops over expert speculation.**\n- **Continuous deployment of small improvements over big-bang releases.**\n\nThese approaches do not replace classical ID. They reframe it. The analysis still happens, but it is broken up and validated chunk by chunk. The evaluation still happens, but it is continuous rather than reserved for the end.\n\nAgile is most powerful in **digital learning at scale** where instrumentation is cheap, iteration is cheap, and learner cohorts are large enough to detect signal. With only 12 learners, an A/B test of two lesson versions cannot reach a reliable conclusion; with 12,000, it can, and Lean ID becomes genuinely data-driven rather than data-decorated.\n\n---\n\n## A Side-by-Side Comparison\n\n| Model | Defining emphasis | Best-fit project | Main limitation |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ADDIE | Shared phase vocabulary | Coordinating mixed teams | Linear if taken literally |\n| Dick & Carey | Systems alignment | High-stakes, auditable content | Heavy for short cycles |\n| Kemp | Non-linear, continuous | Classroom/higher-ed redesign | Less prescriptive for novices |\n| Smith & Ragan | Justified strategy decisions | Teaching design reasoning | Still front-loaded |\n| SAM | Rapid prototyping | Unclear stakeholder needs | Risky for regulated content |\n| Agile / Lean | Validated iteration | Large-scale digital products | Needs telemetry and scale |\n\nBefore you choose a model, prove you can tell them apart by their defining emphasis. Match each model to the one phrase that distinguishes it from the others.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Dick and Carey\",\"definition\":\"Systems alignment: objectives, assessment, and strategy designed in lockstep, drift treated as a defect\"},{\"term\":\"Kemp\",\"definition\":\"Non-linear oval: start at any element, with project management and formative evaluation running continuously\"},{\"term\":\"Smith and Ragan\",\"definition\":\"Splits strategy into organizational, delivery, and management decisions that novices tend to conflate\"},{\"term\":\"SAM\",\"definition\":\"Three iterative phases where decisions are made by testing prototypes, not signing off documents\"},{\"term\":\"Agile / Lean\",\"definition\":\"Ships small learning chunks in sprints and treats each design choice as a hypothesis validated by learner data\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A K-12 teacher redesigning a unit starts at 'content sequencing' because the textbook changed, then loops back to 'objectives' when the new sequence exposes a missing prerequisite. Which model explicitly legitimizes that loop instead of treating it as a process violation?\" explanation=\"The Kemp model arranges its nine elements in an oval, so you can start at any element where you have new information and revisit earlier ones whenever a downstream decision exposes a gap. Dick and Carey enforces a deliberate forward ordering; SAM is iterative but organized around prototyping, not classroom redesign.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The Kemp model\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Dick and Carey\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Pure Agile\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example: Selecting a Model Under Real Constraints\n\n**The brief.** A learning-and-development team must redesign a 4-week leadership pipeline for a regulated healthcare provider. Stakeholders cannot agree on requirements. The final assessment must be defensible to external auditors. The team has 6 weeks and a small pool of subject-matter experts (SMEs). Which model, or combination, should they use?\n\nWork the decision the way a mature designer would, one constraint at a time. Predict each step before revealing it.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Selecting a model under conflicting constraints\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Two risks compete: a regulated assessment auditors will inspect, and stakeholders who cannot specify requirements. Can a single pure model serve both well?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"No, the two risks pull toward opposite model families\",\"Yes, ADDIE covers everything\",\"Yes, SAM covers everything\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"No. Defensibility pulls toward an alignment model (Dick and Carey); ambiguity pulls toward an iterative model (SAM). One pure model serves only one risk.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Which requirement is non-negotiable and therefore governs the assessment and objectives?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Defensibility to auditors\",\"Speed of iteration\",\"Stakeholder comfort\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Defensibility is non-negotiable (auditors), so objectives and assessment must be built with alignment discipline (Dick and Carey).\"},{\"prompt\":\"Where does the genuine ambiguity actually live, so iteration is safe there?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"In the experience design: activities, scenarios, sequencing\",\"In the assessment blueprint\",\"In the compliance sign-off\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"In the experience design. So run that part as SAM iterations against the frozen objective-and-assessment backbone.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Final answer: Dick and Carey as the alignment/governance model, SAM as the delivery model, with the objective-and-assessment backbone frozen before iteration begins. The hidden constraint, a small SME pool, is what makes SAM-style short cycles ration scarce SME attention better than a heavy linear process.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n**Step 1: Name the dominant risks.**\nTwo risks compete. (a) *Accuracy/defensibility:* a regulated assessment that auditors will inspect. (b) *Ambiguity:* stakeholders cannot specify requirements. A single pure model serves only one of these well.\n\n**Step 2: Map each risk to its natural model.**\n\n| Risk in this project | Model that attacks it |\n| --- | --- |\n| Defensible objective-to-assessment alignment | Dick & Carey (or Smith & Ragan) |\n| Requirements that only emerge by building | SAM (iterative prototyping) |\n\n**Step 3: Resolve the conflict instead of picking one.**\nThe defensibility requirement is non-negotiable (auditors), so the *assessment and objectives* must be developed with alignment discipline. The ambiguity requirement is real but lives mostly in the *experience design* (activities, scenarios, sequencing), where iteration is safe. So split the project: govern the assessment with Dick and Carey, and run the experience design as SAM iterations.\n\n**Step 4: Sequence under the time budget (6 weeks).**\n\n- Weeks 1-2: Dick and Carey steps 1-5. Lock instructional goals, instructional analysis, learner/context analysis, performance objectives, and the assessment blueprint. This produces the auditable backbone early.\n- Weeks 2-5: SAM iterative design over the *instructional strategy and materials*, three short prototype-review-revise cycles with the small SME pool, each cycle checked against the locked objectives so iteration cannot drift out of alignment.\n- Week 6: Formative evaluation pass plus a compliance review of the final assessment before any learner sees it.\n\n**Step 5: State the answer and why.**\nUse **Dick and Carey as the alignment/governance model and SAM as the delivery model**, with the objective-and-assessment backbone frozen before iteration begins. This satisfies the auditors (traceable alignment) without paying the full front-loaded cost on the parts of the design that are genuinely uncertain. Pure SAM would risk an indefensible assessment; pure Dick and Carey would burn the 6-week budget specifying activities nobody had validated yet.\n\n> **Why this is the textbook move:** The small SME pool is the hidden constraint. SAM's short cycles ration scarce SME attention better than a heavy linear process that needs SMEs available continuously for deep documentation review.\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"ID Model Selection\" context=\"A learning-and-development team must redesign a 4-week leadership pipeline for a regulated healthcare provider. Stakeholders cannot agree on requirements, but the assessment must be defensible to auditors. The team has 6 weeks and a small SME pool.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Choosing a Model: A Quick Decision Frame\n\nNo model is universally best. Use this frame to choose:\n\n- **High-stakes accuracy, regulated content** -> Dick and Carey or Smith and Ragan\n- **Classroom or higher-ed redesign with shifting requirements** -> Kemp\n- **Corporate microlearning with unclear stakeholder needs** -> SAM\n- **Large-scale digital product with telemetry** -> Agile or Lean\n- **Cross-team shared vocabulary, mixed audience** -> ADDIE as a coordination layer, with one of the above as the working model underneath\n\nMature designers usually run **two models at once**: a defensible alignment model (often Dick and Carey or Smith and Ragan) for evidence and accountability, and a flexible delivery model (SAM or Agile) for execution speed. The worked example above is exactly that pattern in action.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain in your own words why freezing the objective-and-assessment backbone BEFORE iteration begins is what makes the combined Dick-and-Carey-plus-SAM pattern work. What goes wrong if you skip the freeze?\" answer=\"The frozen backbone is the alignment anchor every prototype is checked against, so iteration on activities and scenarios cannot quietly drift away from what the objectives and assessment demand. Without the freeze, SAM iteration has no fixed target: each cycle can redefine what success means, the assessment loses traceability, and the audit defensibility (the whole reason Dick and Carey was chosen) is lost. The freeze lets you iterate safely on the uncertain parts while keeping the auditable parts stable.\"></self-explain>\n\n---\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n- **Treating ADDIE as a linear waterfall.** Writing a complete analysis document before any design, then never revisiting it, recreates the slow front-loaded failure even when the project demanded iteration.\n- **Choosing a model by familiarity, not by risk.** \"We always use X\" ignores the project's dominant risk. Diagnose the risk first, then pick the model.\n- **Iterating without an alignment anchor.** SAM and Agile drift if there is no frozen objective-and-assessment backbone to check each prototype against. Iteration is not an excuse to skip alignment.\n- **Over-modeling small jobs.** A subordinate-skills hierarchy for a 20-minute refresher is wasted budget. Match the model's weight to the project's stakes.\n- **Confusing the model with the deliverable.** The model organizes the work; it is not the course. A beautiful process diagram does not teach anyone anything.\n\n---\n\n## Why Comparing Models Matters\n\nA designer who only knows ADDIE will treat every project the same way. They will write a long Analysis document for a two-week microlearning sprint. They will skip iterative prototyping for an unclear stakeholder. They will leave alignment between objectives and assessment to the end of Development, where it is most expensive to fix.\n\nA designer who knows several models can match the model to the project's risk, audience, and uncertainty. That is the central professional skill of instructional design: **selecting and combining methods**, not loyally executing one.\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"id_models\",\"dick_and_carey_model\",\"kemp_model\",\"smith_and_ragan_model\",\"successive_approximation_model\",\"agile_id\"]'></concept-map>\n\n- ID process models are **lenses**, not recipes; each foregrounds a different concern.\n- Two axes organize the field: **how much is specified up front** and **how change is treated**.\n- **Dick and Carey** and **Smith and Ragan** maximize alignment and defensibility; **SAM** and **Agile/Lean** maximize iteration speed and learner signal; **Kemp** is the structured-but-non-linear middle.\n- Choose the model that **attacks the project's dominant risk first** (accuracy, speed, ambiguity, or feasibility).\n- Mature practice **combines** an alignment model with a delivery model, freezing the objective-and-assessment backbone before iteration begins.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn the next section, we move from choosing a process model to executing its first real activity: **needs and learner analysis**. The model tells you when analysis happens and how heavily; the analysis itself decides whether the rest of the design is aimed at the right target.\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A government safety agency commissions a 6-month course to certify pilots on a new emergency procedure. The content must be auditable and assessment must be defensible. Which ID model approach fits best?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"SAM, because rapid iteration always wins.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Dick and Carey or Smith and Ragan, because alignment of objectives, assessment, and strategy must be explicit and traceable.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Pure Agile, with no formal analysis phase.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. High-stakes regulated training rewards explicit alignment between objectives, assessment, and strategy, which Dick and Carey and Smith and Ragan both make defensible.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## References\n\n- Keller, J. M. (1987). *The systematic process of motivational design.* Performance + Instruction, 26(9), 1–8. https://doi.org/10.1002/pfi.4160260902\n- Bloom, B. S. (1984). *The 2 sigma problem: The search for methods of group instruction as effective as one-to-one tutoring.* Educational Researcher, 13(6), 4–16. https://doi.org/10.3102/0013189X013006004\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"id_models_dnc_strength","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"dick_and_carey_model","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["dick_and_carey_model","id_models"],"stem":"Which feature most distinguishes the Dick, Carey, and Carey model from a generic ADDIE workflow?","options":["It eliminates the need for evaluation.","It treats instruction as a tightly coupled system in which objectives, assessment, and strategy are designed in lockstep with explicit alignment checks.","It only applies to corporate training.","It replaces analysis with rapid prototyping."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Dick and Carey's signature contribution is alignment discipline across objectives, assessment, and strategy — drift between any two is treated as a defect to fix.","misconception_id":"addie_is_the_only_model","learning_objective_ids":["inst-design-02-04-lo-01"]},{"id":"id_models_kemp_pattern","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"kemp_model","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["kemp_model","id_models"],"stem":"What does the oval (rather than linear) layout of the Kemp model communicate about ID work?","options":["That every project must visit every element exactly once.","That the designer can enter at any element with new information and revisit earlier elements as downstream decisions expose gaps.","That evaluation should be skipped if time is short.","That the model only works for K-12."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Kemp's non-linear arrangement reflects how real design work iterates and re-enters earlier elements as the project progresses.","misconception_id":"non_linear_means_unsystematic","learning_objective_ids":["inst-design-02-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"id_models_sam_fit","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"successive_approximation_model","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["successive_approximation_model","agile_id"],"stem":"Which scenario is the strongest fit for the Successive Approximation Model (SAM)?","options":["A regulated medical certification course where a wrong early prototype could mislead trainees.","A corporate microlearning module where stakeholders cannot fully specify requirements until they see something working.","A research grant proposal that requires up-front documentation only.","Any project where iteration is more expensive than over-specification."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"SAM thrives where requirements are uncertain and rework is cheaper than over-specification. High-stakes regulated content is a poor SAM fit.","misconception_id":"sam_replaces_all_analysis","learning_objective_ids":["inst-design-02-04-lo-02"]},{"id":"id_models_combine","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"id_models","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["id_models","smith_and_ragan_model"],"stem":"What does it mean for a mature designer to 'run two models at once'?","options":["Deliver two separate courses simultaneously.","Use a defensible alignment model (e.g., Dick and Carey, Smith and Ragan) to underwrite evidence and accountability while using a flexible delivery model (e.g., SAM or Agile) to execute quickly.","Pick whichever model the stakeholder named first.","Switch models every week regardless of progress."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Combining an alignment-focused model with an execution-focused model is the practical move that gives both rigor and speed.","misconception_id":"one_model_must_win","learning_objective_ids":["inst-design-02-04-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"addie_is_the_only_model","pattern":"model_monoculture","description":"Treating ADDIE as the only legitimate ID model and forcing every project through its five letters","trigger":"learner argues that any deviation from ADDIE is unprofessional","feedback":"ADDIE is one useful coordination layer, not the only valid model. Mature designers select among Dick and Carey, Kemp, Smith and Ragan, SAM, and Agile based on project risk and audience.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"non_linear_means_unsystematic","pattern":"process_rigor_error","description":"Assuming non-linear models like Kemp or Agile are less rigorous than linear models","trigger":"learner equates step-order with quality assurance","feedback":"Rigor lives in the alignment between objectives, assessment, and strategy — not in the order in which elements are visited. Non-linear models can be fully rigorous.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"sam_replaces_all_analysis","pattern":"phase_skipping_error","description":"Believing SAM and Agile eliminate the need for any analysis","trigger":"learner says 'just prototype, skip analysis'","feedback":"SAM and Agile compress and distribute analysis across iterations; they do not remove it. Each prototype is itself an analysis instrument.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"one_model_must_win","pattern":"false_dichotomy","description":"Assuming a project must commit to exactly one ID model","trigger":"learner asks which single model is 'best'","feedback":"Real design work usually layers two models: an alignment model for evidence and accountability, and a delivery model for execution speed. Both can run in parallel.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"d667ac51b15915632166e0ef68b19efa0921e83e42625464cc736d773d2bf48d","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}